  "client_view_text": " In your explanation, assume a database has a write-ahead log (WAL) and a shared buffer cache. \u2022\nA This is also referred to as a \"crash recovery\" mechanism.\nA write-ahead log, also known as a redo log, is a mechanism used by a database to ensure data consistency and recoverability. The log is a record of all the changes made to the database, in the order they were made. It acts as a journal of all write operations to the database, and is used to recover the database in the event of a crash.\nWhen a write operation is made to the database, the database first writes the change to the log, and then applies the change to the database. This ensures that the log is always up to date and that the database is consistent with the log. If the database crashes or is shut down, the log provides a record of all the changes that were made but not yet committed to the database. By replaying the log, the database can recover to a consistent state, even if the original data was lost.\nReplaying the log involves reading the log from the start and applying each change to the database, in the order they were made. This ensures that the database is restored to a consistent state, even if the original data was incomplete or corrupted. The database can then continue to operate, using the recovered data as its base. This mechanism"
